<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hong Kong Island</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> Island is an island on the south side of Victoria Harbour, at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pearl-river">Pearl River</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1841 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a> and ceded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nanjing">Treaty of Nanjing</a> in 1842, it became the core of the colony of Hong Kong, with its capital at Victoria. It returned to Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> with the rest of Hong Kong in 1997.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hong-kong-island.jpg" alt="Hong Kong Island" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: momo from Hong Kong · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hong_Kong_Island_Skyline_201108.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hong Kong Island is the second largest island in Hong Kong, after Lantau, with a land area of about 78.59 square kilometres. It was known originally, and on road signs, simply as Hong Kong. Its northern coast forms the southern shore of Victoria Harbour, whose deep waters, favoured by large trading ships, were largely responsible for the development of Hong Kong. The island is one of the three areas of Hong Kong, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kowloon">Kowloon</a> and the New Territories, and is often called the Hong Kong side or Island side.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human settlement in the area dates back some 6,000 years, as shown by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> artefacts found at Stanley. In 214 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baiyue">Baiyue</a> and absorbed their lands, including the island, which came under Panyu County in the Nanhai Commandery. Copper coins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a>, Tang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> dynasties unearthed near the site of the Royal Hong Kong Yacht Club suggest commercial activity in those periods. In 1573 the island belonged to Xin'an County.</p><p>In 1661 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shunzhi-emperor">Shunzhi Emperor</a> ordered the great clearance, which forced people in coastal areas to move inland to keep Ming loyalists in Formosa from reaching the shores. The ban was lifted in 1669, though few of the original residents returned, and piracy was rampant until the cession of 1842.</p><p>After the Qing defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a> the island was ceded to the United Kingdom in perpetuity under the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanking</a> in 1842, and British forces established the City of Victoria, named for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>. The Battle of Hong Kong began on 8 December 1941, eight hours after the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a>. Defenders from British, Canadian and Indian forces and the Hong Kong Volunteer Defence Forces held the Wong Nai Chung Gap for a time, but the British surrendered on 25 December 1941, and the Governor, Mark Young, gave himself up at the Peninsula Hotel. The population of Hong Kong fell from 1.6 million in 1941 to 600,000 in 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the population of the island grew rapidly, and areas such as North Point, Shau Kei Wan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a> were developed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a> over the island passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> on 1 July 1997, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kowloon">Kowloon</a> and the New Territories. The Central area in the north-east remains the historical, political and economic centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>, with the Government House, the Legislative Council Complex and the Hong Kong Stock Exchange. The Peak is among its best-known sights, and the island's mountains are popular for hiking.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hong Kong Island” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chinese markets were forced open through the Opium Wars and the unequal treaty system, which granted foreign powers extraterritorial jurisdiction and treaty ports, fixed low import tariffs and ceded <b>Hong Kong Island</b> to Britain in 1842.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The First Opium War (1839–1842) had ended in the Treaty of Nanjing, which ceded <b>Hong Kong Island</b> to Britain and opened five ports to foreign trade.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>China ceded <b>Hong Kong Island</b> to Britain, opened five ports to foreign trade and paid a large indemnity.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-29">29 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>During the First Opium War, Charles Elliot and the Qing envoy Qishan agreed terms ceding <b>Hong Kong Island</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-20">20 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>British forces occupied <b>Hong Kong Island</b> in January 1841 during the First Opium War, and the Treaty of Nanking ceded it to Britain in 1842.</blockquote><a href="/state/hong-kong-british">Hong Kong (British)</a></li></ul></section>
